**Code Explanation:**

The code performs the statistical validation and visualization of the Information Ratio (IR) decay for the OLS-FMP and Characteristic-Sorted portfolios. 1. Newey-West Standard Errors: We implement a function newey_west_se to calculate the standard error of the mean return for each portfolio, accounting for potential autocorrelation using a lag of 3 months. 2. Bootstrap Analysis: We use a block bootstrap approach (block_size=3, n_boot=1000) to generate 95% confidence intervals for the IRs of both the OLS-FMP and Characteristic-Sorted portfolios, as well as for the difference between their IRs (Δ IR = IR_OLS - IR_Char). This allows us to statistically test whether the structural simplicity of characteristic sorting outperforms the statistical complexity of OLS in small-N environments. 3. Noise-to-Signal Ratio (NSR): We calculate the NSR for the OLS-FMP as the variance of the noise component divided by the variance of the factor component (Var(R_noise) / Var(R_factor)). This quantifies the idiosyncratic volatility leakage. 4. Visualizations: The first plot is a 2x2 grid. The top row displays the IR decay for SMB and WML factors across different cross-sectional sizes (N), overlaying the 95% bootstrap confidence intervals directly as shaded regions. The bottom row plots the mean IR difference between OLS and Characteristic-Sorted portfolios along with its 95% confidence interval, clearly showing the threshold where one method becomes statistically superior to the other (i.e., where the CI crosses zero). The second plot visualizes the Noise-to-Signal Ratio on a logarithmic scale as a function of N for both SMB and WML factors. 5. Output: All quantitative metrics, including standard errors, confidence intervals, and NSRs, are printed to the console for the researcher to interpret. The results are also saved to step_5_data.pkl.

**Modifications:**

Added if __name__ == '__main__': block, ensured all file paths use the data/ directory, removed LaTeX rendering, and ensured no command-line arguments are used.

